## Most Likely Causes & Solutions

### 1. **Dependencies are Objects/Arrays (Reference Issues)**

The most common cause is passing objects or arrays as dependencies. Even if the values are identical, React compares by reference.

```jsx
// ❌ Problem: Object reference changes on every render
const [user, setUser] = useState({ name: 'John', age: 30 });

useEffect(() => {
  console.log('User changed:', user);
}, [user]); // This will run every render!

// ✅ Solution: Use primitive values or memoize the object
const user = { name: 'John', age: 30 };

useEffect(() => {
  console.log('User changed:', user);
}, [user.name, user.age]); // Only re-runs when name or age changes

// Or use useMemo to stabilize the object
const stableUser = useMemo(() => ({ name: 'John', age: 30 }), []);
useEffect(() => {
  console.log('User changed:', stableUser);
}, [stableUser]);
```

